Inlaw Biker wrote:
> It depends.  Certain classes have a big head start to playing this way,
> such as healer and archaeologist.

Archaeologists are one of the worst to try it with, IMO.  They can
already have plenty of cash for the first several protection purchases
without limiting their levels.  So then you're talking about going
through the first 6-8 levels with a handful of HP in order to have
_maybe_ 2-3 more AC for the next 6-8 levels.

>  A base armor class of 1 or 0 is a huge advantage

Sure, but everyone will have that pretty soon anyone, regardless of the
racket, and many of them will be able to buy 1 level of protection (the
many AC for one cost level) when they reach the mines anyway.  The
question is whether having just a few hp for the hardest part of the
game is a reasonable tradeoff to make in exchange for having somewhere
between 6-10 extra AC for the next few levels, after which everyone has
the same AC anyway.

If it works for you, that's great.  In fact, since I'm running a
pacifist healer now I'll be trying it.  But it's not a good strategy
unless you're playing pacifist, IMO--I'd go so far as to say that a
character is much more likely to  get 10 ac of protection if they don't
do the protection racket than if they do.



